# Cheapest route planner.
#
# Reads INPUT/routes.txt, prints the lexicographically smallest cheapest route
# for each trip, in input order.
#
# Method: for each destination Y, run Dijkstra from Y over the undirected graph
# to get dist_to_Y(v) for every town v. Then walk from X, at each step taking
# the smallest neighbour v with cost(cur, v) + dist_to_Y(v) == dist_to_Y(cur).
# Every such choice keeps the rest of the route optimal, so picking the
# smallest one at each step yields the smallest town list among the cheapest
# routes. The walk never recomputes costs.
#
# This does not allocate the 9000 x 9000 table the maintainer notes ask for.
# That table would be about 81 million Python list slots (roughly 650 MB of
# pointers alone), which would exceed the memory limit. It is also not needed:
# the distance-to-target walk gives the same tie-break result. Names are kept
# as the original strings, and str comparison in Python is code-point order,
# which matches UTF-8 byte order.

